# Kabyle am surfaces.
# "n tufat" is CLDR 47's dayPeriods am for kab (cldr-dates-full/main/kab/
# ca-gregorian.json), and kab.wikipedia writes it in running text:
#   "Di 7 n tufat n wass n 8 yennayer" (Imenɣi n Lezzayer (1956-1957))
# The commonest running-text form is the "morning" noun in the genitive frame
# "n t(a)ṣebḥit", spelled on kab.wikipedia with the emphatic ṣ, matching
# daypart_morning.voc:
#   "ɣef lweḥda n tṣ̣ebḥit : tebda-d temhelt n Mizrana" (Tafsut n Yimaziɣen)
#   "Lɛecṛa n tṣ̣ebḥit. Ldzayer tamaneɣt, deg Tsenbert ..." (idem)
# The plain-s "ssbaḥ" is attested too:
#   "Neffeɣ-d ɣef lxemsa n ssbaḥ" (Agarsafen, quoted song text)
# "ssbeḥ", the surface this file shipped with, has no attestation of its own.
# It is kept as a tolerated variant rather than dropped: it collides with no
# other Kabyle word, so accepting it costs nothing, while the emphatic ṣṣbeḥ
# added above is what real Kabyle writes and what the rest of the locale uses.
n tufat
tufat
n tṣebḥit
n tṣ̣ebḥit
n taṣebḥit
ṣṣbeḥ
taṣebḥit
n ssbaḥ
ssbaḥ
ssbeḥ
